# Getting started

An Upwell application is ordinary Rust: types you declare, plus the wiring Upwell generates around them. You describe *what* the application is made of — components, controllers or services, configuration — and the framework owns dependency resolution, the process entry point, configuration loading, and the shutdown lifecycle. Nothing here requires additional tooling; `cargo run` is enough.

This guide builds one HTTP application end to end. The same three steps — declare a component, expose it through a protocol, name the host — apply to an RPC daemon or a background worker.

## Create the project

There are two ways to start, and the rest of this guide is identical either way. Cargo alone needs nothing but the framework crate. `cargo upwell init` generates the same shape from a template, including the host declaration, a configuration file, and the library and binary split — useful once, not required.

{#snippet withCargo()}

<Example title="Start with Cargo alone">

```sh
cargo new catalog-api
cd catalog-api
cargo add upwell --features axum
```

Protocols are features: `axum` for HTTP, `daemon` for the native RPC protocol, or both. Three more direct dependencies are needed because generated code names them rather than reaching them through Upwell — the async runtime that starts the process, `serde` for the types that cross the wire, and `clap` for the command line the default `cli` feature generates.

```sh
cargo add tokio --features macros,rt-multi-thread
cargo add serde --features derive
cargo add clap --features derive
```

A transitive dependency is not enough for any of the three: the code Upwell generates writes `::clap`, `::serde`, and `::tokio` paths into your crate, which only resolve against its own dependencies.

</Example>

{/snippet}

{#snippet withTooling()}

<Example title="Scaffold with Cargo Upwell">

```sh
cargo install cargo-upwell
```

Generate a project from the built-in application template. Naming the template keeps the command reproducible; without it, the tool selects one interactively:

```sh
cargo upwell init catalog-api --template upwell/application
cd catalog-api
```

The generated project already declares its host, its dependencies, and its `application.toml`, so it builds and serves before you write anything:

```sh
cargo run
```

</Example>

{/snippet}

<Tabs labels={['Cargo only', 'cargo upwell init']} panels={[withCargo, withTooling]} />

The `cli` and `di-check` features are enabled by default. `cli` generates the process runner and argument parser for a named application; `di-check` turns a missing dependency into a `cargo check` error instead of a startup failure. Add `jobs` for scheduled work, `ws` or `stomp` for WebSocket protocols, and `client` to generate typed clients.

The framework never requires the tool: `cargo build` and `cargo run` are the only commands this guide needs. See <GuideRef slug="cargo-upwell">Cargo Upwell</GuideRef> for what it adds afterwards — validation, inspection, and the other project templates.

The sections below write the application from nothing. If you scaffolded one instead, read them as a tour of the code the template generated.

## Declare a component

A component is a singleton the container constructs once and injects wherever it is declared as a field. Write the type and its behavior as you normally would; <Symbol path="upwell::prelude::component" label="#[component]" /> registers it and derives its factory from its fields.

<Example title="A greeting component">

```rust
use std::sync::Arc;
use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicU64, Ordering};

use upwell::prelude::*;

#[component(by_value)]
#[derive(Clone)]
struct Greeter {
    #[default]
    greetings: Arc<AtomicU64>,
}
```

Behavior stays in an ordinary inherent implementation — the attribute changes how the type is *constructed*, not how it is written:

```rust
impl Greeter {
    fn greet(&self, who: &str) -> (String, u64) {
        let count = self.greetings.fetch_add(1, Ordering::Relaxed) + 1;

        (format!("Hello, {who}!"), count)
    }
}
```

</Example>

Consumers usually inject `Arc<T>` and share one value. `by_value` additionally allows the component to be injected as an owned clone, which is what a controller field needs. `#[default]` supplies a field the container cannot resolve from another component.

## Expose it through the protocol

A protocol turns components into a served surface. For HTTP, a controller groups routes under one base path and declares its dependencies as fields.

<Example title="A controller over the component">

```rust
use upwell::axum::dto;
use upwell::axum::prelude::*;
use upwell::prelude::*;

#[dto]
struct Greeting {
    message: String,
    count: u64,
}
```

The controller is a singleton like any other component, so `greeter` is resolved for it once:

```rust
#[controller(path = "/greet")]
struct GreetController {
    greeter: Greeter,
}
```

Routes are methods in a `#[handlers]` block. Axum extractors supply request data; `Json<T>` writes a typed response:

```rust
#[handlers]
impl GreetController {
    #[get("/{who}")]
    async fn greet(&self, Path(who): Path<String>) -> Json<Greeting> {
        let (message, count) = self.greeter.greet(&who);

        Json(Greeting { message, count })
    }
}
```

</Example>

`#[dto]` marks a type as wire data and derives its serialization. Controllers register themselves at link time, so no route table is maintained by hand.

## Name the host

`app!` declares the application host: one name, one protocol, and the lifecycle phase that starts serving. It generates the entry point, argument parsing, and startup and shutdown orchestration that a hand-written `main` would otherwise own.

<Example title="The generated host">

```rust
use upwell::axum::Axum;
use upwell::axum::prelude::*;
use upwell::prelude::*;

app! {
    app CatalogApplication {
        name: "catalog-api",
        protocol: Axum,
        serve(_context, app) {
            app.serve_configured().await
        },
    }
}
```

`main` hands the process to that host and nothing else:

```rust
#[tokio::main]
async fn main() -> Result<(), upwell::CliError> {
    CatalogApplication::run().await
}
```

</Example>

The host discovers linked declarations, so `Greeter` and `GreetController` need no registration list. `serve_configured()` binds the listener described by the protocol's own configuration rather than a hardcoded address.

<Callout type="info" title="An item, not an expression">

`app!` declares a type. Do not assign it to a variable or invoke it inside `main`; when a process must own its own startup, assemble the application with `App::<Axum>::builder(...)` instead. See <GuideRef slug="framework">the application model</GuideRef> for that path.

</Callout>

## Run it

<Symbol path="upwell::axum::Axum" /> gives every configuration field an environment-aware default, so the application serves on `127.0.0.1:3000` without a configuration file.

<Example title="Serve the application">

```sh
cargo run
```

```sh
curl localhost:3000/greet/world
```

Override the listener in `application.toml` beside the binary, or with the matching `AXUM_*` environment variables:

```toml
[axum]
bind = "0.0.0.0"
port = 8080
```

</Example>

The generated CLI is part of the host: `cargo run -- --help` lists the commands and arguments this application accepts. A failure to resolve a dependency has already been reported by `cargo check`, not here.

## Where to go next

- <GuideRef slug="framework">Application model</GuideRef> — the assembled <code>App&lt;P&gt;</code>, its lifecycle, and when to use the builder instead of a generated host.
- <GuideRef slug="framework/dependency-injection/components">Components and injection</GuideRef> — construction, providers, and lifetimes beyond a plain singleton.
- <GuideRef slug="axum/http">HTTP controllers</GuideRef> — extraction, request scope, and streaming responses.
- <GuideRef slug="framework/application/app-macro">The <code>app!</code> macro</GuideRef> — configuration callbacks, plugins, and CLI commands on the host.
- <GuideRef slug="cargo-upwell">Cargo Upwell</GuideRef> — the optional command-line tool that validates and inspects an application.

<Callout type="info" title="Project layout">

Once the application grows, keep controllers and components in `lib.rs` and leave `main.rs` as the bootstrap. The library half is what compiles to a browser client, and the split costs nothing before then.

</Callout>
